{
  "dom_id": "step-q90637",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/8e0125007c4384cdd3eb5d939176b2d1ede7a6525da26fb5e99ccfe67e4077a9.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=3$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=1$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function has a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph and our function does *not* have points where its value is greater than $f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/c288ece1ca29e5da8ec8d64583b25f7538a929a3de295c24be6396339649d2d4.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=1.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greatest than $f(1).$ For example, $f(4)>f(1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
